    "reasoning": "Confirmed against memlab's installed source rather than its docs. `runGrowth()` (analyze.ts:174) gets the *filtered* list from `listSnapshots()` and uses it for the header (line 179), `newest` (178), and the constructor-delta table (211-212) — but line 186 hands memlab the raw directory: `analysis.analyzeSnapshotsInDirectory(SNAPSHOT_DIR, { workDir: WORK_DIR })`. In `node_modules/@memlab/heap-analysis/dist/BaseAnalysis.js`, `analyzeSnapshotsInDirectory` passes the argument straight through as `args['snapshot-dir']`; `ShapeUnboundGrowthAnalysis.process` calls `PluginUtils.snapshotMapReduce`, which resolves `getSnapshotDirForAnalysis(options)` → that same dir → `utils.getSnapshotFilesInDir(snapshotDir)`, which in `@memlab/core/dist/lib/Utils.js:1436-1447` is a bare `readdirSync(dir).filter(f => f.endsWith('.heapsnapshot'))`. The JSDoc is explicit: 'all snapshot files will be loaded and analyzed'. So the `--sha` / `--latest-version` filter has literally no effect on the shape analysis, while `listSnapshots()` has already printed `Filtering to version <sha>: N/M snapshots`. The result is exactly the described mismatch: a filtered header and constructor table printed over an unfiltered shape series, with `s.counts` having one entry per snapshot in the whole directory, which `classifyTrend` then midpoint-slices — so cross-version shape churn is reported as sustained growth in the current build. The flags were added (per memtest/README.md:85-89, 162-169) precisely for the multi-SHA retained window, which is the case that is now wrong. BUG is right: internal analysis tooling, no production impact, but a report that actively misleads."
