    "reasoning": "The finding filed this at medium confidence; I was able to settle the decisive mechanism, and it holds. Statically: `COMPRESSIBLE_TYPES[0]` is the bare `'text/'` prefix and `isCompressible` is a `startsWith` test, so `text/event-stream` matches and line 67 pipes the body through `new CompressionStream(COMPRESSION_FORMAT[chosen])` with the default `methods: ['zstd', 'gzip']`. I then pulled Bun's own implementation (commit 5702b39, `src/js/builtins/CompressionStream.ts`): `CompressionStream` is a thin wrapper — `zlib.createGzip()` / `zlib.createZstdCompress()` bridged via `stream.Readable.toWeb` / `Writable.toWeb`. In Bun's `src/js/node/zlib.ts` the defaults are `zlibDefaultOpts = { flush: Z_NO_FLUSH, finishFlush: Z_FINISH }` (line 672) and `zstdDefaultOpts = { flush: ZSTD_e_continue, finishFlush: ZSTD_e_end }` (line 909); `ZlibBase.prototype._transform` (line 403) uses `_defaultFlushFlag` for every chunk and only raises to `_finishFlushFlag` for the final one, and `processCallback` pushes output only under `if (have > 0)` (line 571). So a ~30-byte frame mid-stream yields zero pushed bytes — there is no per-chunk flush, confirming the claim for Bun specifically, not just from the WHATWG algorithm. This also explains why the existing regression test is not coverage: gzip emits its 10-byte header on the first `deflate()` call, which alone satisfies `expect(first.value!.byteLength).toBeGreaterThan(0)` — 16 KB of `'head'.repeat(4096)` produces only ~68 LZ77 symbols, nowhere near a block flush. I verified the surrounding reachability claims too: `Mochi.sse()` (Mochi.ts:1330-1432) returns its Response directly with no `middleware` call, while `Mochi.api()` (line 1240) and `Mochi.page()` (line 991) run the chain, and `isDev` (`utils/env.ts`, a live `export let` seeded from `NODE_ENV === 'development'`) makes the middleware a no-op in dev — so a consumer sees a working stream locally and a stalled one in production. Current in-repo impact is nil: `grep 'new ReadableStream' packages/*/src` finds nothing, and the only SSE is `Mochi.sse('/sse/time')`, which bypasses the chain — so this is a latent bug for `mochi-framework` npm consumers, which is why I keep it at BUG rather than raising it. Two minor corrections: `application/x-ndjson` does *not* match `COMPRESSIBLE_TYPES`, so the affected set is really 'any `text/*` body streamed in small chunks' (a `text/plain` token feed included), and the L22 doc comment's streaming guarantee is only true for large chunks."
